<!-- question: 2 -->
  <question type="stack">
    <name><text>Biostatistics - Q2: Experimental Design Concepts</text></name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Answer the following questions regarding experimental design:</p>

<p><strong>1. Power in Split-Plot Design:</strong> In a Split-Plot design investigating "Irrigation (A)" and "Nitrogen (B)", why is the F-test for Factor A usually less powerful than Factor B?</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>

<p><strong>2. Latin Square Design (LSD):</strong> For an LSD with {@t@} treatments, calculate the degrees of freedom for Rows, Trt, and Error:</p>
<p>Rows df = [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<p>Trt df = [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>
<p>Error df = [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]]</p>

<p><strong>3. Coefficient of Variation (CV):</strong> Calculate the CV (as a percentage) if \( MSE = {@MSE_val@} \), the mean \( \bar{Y} = {@mean_val@} \), and the number of replicates is accounted for in the MSE.</p>
<p>CV (%) = [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] %</p>
<p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p><strong>1. Split-Plot Power:</strong> Factor A (Main Plot) is tested against <strong>Error (a)</strong>, which is based on fewer replications and larger experimental units. Error (a) is typically larger than Error (b), making the test for Factor A less sensitive (less power).</p>
<p><strong>2. LSD df (t={@t@}):</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Rows df = \( t - 1 = {@t-1@} \)</li>
  <li>Trt df = \( t - 1 = {@t-1@} \)</li>
  <li>Error df = \( (t-1)(t-2) = ({@t-1@})({@t-2@}) = {@ta4@} \)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. CV Calculation:</strong></p>
<p>\[ CV = \frac{\sqrt{MSE}}{\bar{Y}} \times 100 = \frac{\sqrt{{@MSE_val@}}}{{@mean_val@}} \times 100 \approx {@ta5@} \% \]</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>ta1: [[1, true, "Factor A is tested against Error (a), which is typically larger than Error (b)"], [2, false, "Factor A has more replications than Factor B"], [3, false, "Factor B is tested against the total variance"]];

t: 5;
ta2: t - 1;
ta3: t - 1;
ta4: (t-1)*(t-2);

MSE_val: 4.5;
mean_val: 20;
ta5: float((sqrt(MSE_val)/mean_val)*100);</text>
    </questionvariables>
    <specificfeedback format="html"><text></text></specificfeedback>
    <questionnote format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>LSD df={@ta4@}, CV={@ta5@}%</p>]]></text></questionnote>
